<partial name="_VisualMcpDocsNav" />

<header class="page-head docs-head">
    <div>
        <h1>Refactoring and style</h1>
        <p class="lede">
            VisualMCP can change your code, not just read it. This guide
            explains what makes those changes different from a find/replace,
            which tools preview before writing, and how to run the style
            engine without surprises.
        </p>
    </div>
</header>

<div class="docs-body prose">
    <h2>What “semantic refactoring” means here</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Resolves the symbol</strong> through Roslyn — the exact declaration, overload or override you named.</li>
        <li><strong>Rewrites the affected references</strong> across the solution — call sites, interface implementations, usings, constructor calls.</li>
        <li><strong>Refuses rather than guesses</strong> when the operation would change meaning or cannot be expressed: a method group in <code>nameof</code>, an object initializer, a base class that uses the member.</li>
        <li><strong>Records the write</strong> in the solution's edit history, with the previous text, so it can be audited and reverted.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
        A rename through <code>apply_rename</code> updates the declaration and
        every real reference. A find/replace renames the same text in
        comments, strings and unrelated types — and usually misses the
        implicit interface implementations.
    </p>

    <h2>The safety ladder</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Read-only previews</strong></td><td><code>preview_rename</code>, <code>extract_method_candidates</code>, <code>inspect_code</code> — see the shape before touching anything.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Dry run</strong></td><td>Most write tools accept <code>dryRun</code>: a before/after pair and a line diff per edit, nothing written.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Compiler gate</strong></td><td>Risky operations compile the project after the change and refuse it if it would add errors.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Edit history</strong></td><td><code>edit_history</code> lists every write; <code>revert_edit(id)</code> restores the file exactly.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Your own safety net</strong></td><td>A branch and a clean <code>git_status</code> are still the cheapest rollback.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The catalogue by task</h2>
    <h3>Rename and move</h3>
    <p>
        <code>apply_rename</code> / <code>preview_rename</code>,
        <code>rename_namespace</code>, <code>move_type</code>,
        <code>move_to_namespace</code>, <code>move_static_members</code>,
        <code>move_instance_method</code>. Renames are semantic: parameter
        names, backing fields, <code>nameof</code>, XML docs and interface
        members follow.
    </p>
    <h3>Extract and inline</h3>
    <p>
        <code>extract_method</code>, <code>extract_class</code>,
        <code>extract_superclass</code>, <code>extract_interface</code>,
        <code>inline_symbol</code>, <code>introduce_variable</code>.
        <code>extract_method</code> handles a contiguous statement block; a
        block that awaits becomes an <code>async Task</code> method, and
        blocks that cannot be proven equivalent — <code>yield</code>,
        <code>break</code>, a mid-block return — are refused.
    </p>
    <h3>Signatures and types</h3>
    <p>
        <code>change_signature</code> is the one to reach for when a parameter
        is added, removed or reordered: it returns every call site as a
        before/after pair and is gated on the compiler.
        <code>change_type</code>, <code>change_nullability</code>,
        <code>use_base_type</code>, <code>convert_to_record</code>,
        <code>convert_to_primary_constructor</code>,
        <code>constructor_to_factory</code>, <code>copy_type</code>.
    </p>
    <h3>Inheritance and membership</h3>
    <p>
        <code>pull_members_up</code>, <code>push_members_down</code>,
        <code>generate_members</code>, <code>implement_inpc</code>,
        <code>make_static</code>, <code>type_to_partial</code>. A selection
        that would not compile is refused with the name of the offending
        member, not applied half-way.
    </p>
    <h3>Complexity</h3>
    <p>
        <code>reduce_complexity</code> is the bridge between analysis and
        refactoring: it plans the moves that remove decisions from a method —
        extract a nested block, name a tangled boolean, flatten a tail
        <code>if</code> into a guard clause — applies them one at a time,
        re-measures, and stops at the target. <code>fix_complexity</code> is
        the same reducer without having to name a method: it ranks the whole
        solution and lowers the worst methods.
    </p>

    <h2>The style engine</h2>
    <p>
        <code>simplify_code</code> applies the IDE's mechanical suggestions —
        the quick-fix popup equivalents — without an IDE. It is a rewrite
        engine over Roslyn syntax trees, not a formatter: collection
        expressions, null-coalescing, pattern matching, expression-bodied
        members, switch expressions, LINQ chain simplifications, dead code and
        unused members, <code>^</code> and ranges, <code>nameof</code>,
        nullability-directive cleanup.
    </p>
    <pre><code>simplify_code scope=project projectName=MyApp rules=IDE0028,IDE0041</code></pre>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Scope</strong> is one document, one project or the solution; <strong>rules</strong> is a comma-separated list, or all supported rules when omitted.</li>
        <li><strong><code>dryRun=true</code> is the default</strong>: the before/after pair per edit and a line diff, nothing written.</li>
        <li>On write, each changed document is <strong>compiled</strong>; a document whose change would add errors is reported under <code>refused</code> and left untouched.</li>
        <li>Team preferences are <strong>option-driven</strong>: the rules that depend on a style choice act only when your <code>.editorconfig</code> sets the option, and the result names the option it waited for.</li>
        <li>Visual Basic has its own layer, <code>simplify_vb_code</code>, with the same shape and its own rule set.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Inspections and code fixes</h2>
    <p>
        Two tools, two directions. <code>inspect_code</code> runs the built-in
        inspection packs — the house rules, Roslynator, .NET analyzers,
        AsyncFixer, Meziantou, StyleCop — over every project, with no analyzer
        package referenced by your solution. <code>apply_code_fix</code>
        re-runs the packs and executes the analyzer's <strong>own</strong>
        code fix provider: preview by default, FixAll supported, and rules
        that ship no fix are refused with that message rather than a guessed
        edit.
    </p>
    <pre><code>inspect_code    packs=all minSeverity=warning ids=RCS*,CA18*
apply_code_fix  diagnosticId=RCS1060 filePath=Services/OrderService.cs line=42</code></pre>

    <h2>What “safe” does not mean</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>The compiler gate proves <strong>compilation, not behaviour</strong>. A rewrite can compile and still change meaning.</li>
        <li>The style gate is per document: one bad rewrite refuses that document's changes; other documents in the same run still apply.</li>
        <li>Refactorings refuse instead of approximating. A refusal names what blocked it; the next step is usually to prepare the code, then retry.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A safe working loop</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><code>git_status</code> — know what is already modified.</li>
        <li>Understand the target: <code>find_symbol</code>, <code>read_symbol</code>, <code>find_references</code>.</li>
        <li>Preview: <code>preview_rename</code>, <code>simplify_code</code> with <code>dryRun=true</code>.</li>
        <li>Write one operation at a time.</li>
        <li><code>build_project</code> and <code>run_tests</code>.</li>
        <li><code>inspect_code</code> to catch what the compiler does not.</li>
        <li>If something is wrong: <code>edit_history</code>, then <code>revert_edit(id)</code>.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

